ovation, and its cascading effects, we can understand how the printing press became an indispensable engine of the Scientific Revolution.\n\n## Historical Context: The World Before the Press\n\nTo appreciate the revolutionary nature of Gutenberg's invention, one must first understand the intellectual and material conditions of fifteenth-century Europe. Before the printing press, books were produced by hand, a laborious process that confined book production to monasteries, scriptoria, and, later, professional scribes and copyists. A single copy of a manuscript could take months or even years to complete, making books extraordinarily expensive and rare. This scarcity had profound consequences for the accumulation and transmission of knowledge.\n\nManuscripts were also prone to errors. Each copy introduced the possibility of transcription mistakes, and without the ability to produce identical copies, errors could compound across generations. Scholars had limited access to the works of their predecessors, and the geographic spread of ideas was slow and unreliable. Scientific knowledge, in particular, suffered because accurate transmission of measurements, diagrams, and mathematical formulas was nearly impossible through manual reproduction.\n\nThe intellectual climate of medieval Europe was dominated by scholasticism, which sought to reconcile classical philosophy\u2014particularly the works of Aristotle and the Church Fathers\u2014with Christian theology. While this tradition preserved and transmitted ancient knowledge, it was often more concerned with authoritative interpretation than with empirical observation and experimentation. The tools"}